-- Q3088: Risk Management and Economics [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["VaR captures tail risk fully because the $5 million figure represents the maximum possible single-day loss","CVaR is always lower than VaR because averaging tail losses produces a smaller number than the threshold","VaR and CVaR are identical measures differing only in name, both calculating average expected portfolio loss","VaR shows only the tail threshold; CVaR averages losses beyond it, revealing severity that VaR ignores"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815-10-50-4A, VaR at the 95% level states there is a 5% chance of losing more than $5 million, but it reveals nothing about loss magnitude beyond that point. CVaR (Expected Shortfall) calculates the average loss conditional on exceeding VaR, providing a severity measure. Two portfolios with identical VaR can have vastly different tail risk profiles.
Wrong (A): VaR is a threshold, not a maximum; actual losses can far exceed the VaR figure.
Wrong (B): CVaR is always higher than VaR because it averages losses that exceed the VaR threshold.
Wrong (C): VaR and CVaR measure fundamentally different aspects of risk distribution and are not interchangeable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3088;

