-- Q3119: Advanced Revenue and Intangibles [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The customer has prepaid for the goods before the agreed delivery date","The seller has shipped the goods to a third-party warehouse for storage","The arrangement is substantive, the product is identified and ready, and the entity cannot redirect it","Revenue on bill-and-hold arrangements can never be recognized before physical delivery"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-55-83, bill-and-hold revenue recognition requires: (1) a substantive reason, typically customer-requested; (2) the product is separately identified as belonging to the customer; (3) the product is currently ready for transfer; and (4) the entity cannot use or direct the product to another customer.
Wrong (A): Prepayment alone does not satisfy bill-and-hold criteria; the arrangement must meet all four specific conditions.
Wrong (B): Shipping to a warehouse is not bill-and-hold; bill-and-hold means the seller retains goods at the customer''s request.
Wrong (D): ASC 606 explicitly permits pre-delivery recognition when all bill-and-hold criteria are met.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3119;

-- Q3121: Advanced Revenue and Intangibles [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the time value of money and recognize full revenue at delivery","Defer all revenue recognition until the customer''s payment is actually received","Adjust the transaction price for time value and recognize interest revenue separately","Record the full contract amount as revenue at delivery with no financing adjustment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-32-15, when payment timing differs significantly from performance, the entity must adjust the transaction price to reflect the time value of money. Revenue is recognized at the adjusted amount at delivery, with interest revenue recognized separately over the financing period.
Wrong (A): Ignoring time value when a significant financing component exists violates ASC 606''s measurement requirements.
Wrong (B): Deferring all revenue until payment contradicts the accrual principle; revenue is earned at delivery, not at collection.
Wrong (D): Recording the full unadjusted amount overstates revenue by including the financing component in the transaction price.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3121;

-- Q3131: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All three dates produce the same expense because stock option value is fixed at issuance","The exercise date is the correct measurement date because compensation is exchanged then","ASC 718 uses the vesting date because that is when the employee earns the right to options","The grant date is the measurement date, producing the lowest but most reliable expense measure"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 718 establishes the grant date as the measurement date for equity-classified awards because it represents the point of mutual commitment. Fair value at grant ($15/option) is lower than vesting-date intrinsic value ($40) or exercise-date intrinsic value ($45), but it provides the most reliable and consistent measure.
Wrong (A): Option value changes significantly over time as stock price, volatility, and time to expiration change.
Wrong (B): The exercise date was used under the obsolete APB 25 intrinsic value method, not under current ASC 718.
Wrong (C): ASC 718 explicitly selects the grant date, not the vesting date, as the measurement date for equity awards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3131;

-- Q3133: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Have no value unless the stock price exceeds a specified exercise price threshold","Can only be granted to executive-level employees under SEC compensation rules","Must be exercised within 90 days of the vesting date or they expire worthless","Have value regardless of stock price movement and settle in shares upon vesting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 718-10-30, RSUs represent a promise to deliver shares upon vesting with grant-date fair value typically equal to the stock price. RSUs retain value regardless of stock price movement because the employee receives the full share value at vesting, unlike options which can become worthless.
Wrong (A): This describes stock options, which have an exercise price and can become underwater; RSUs have no exercise price.
Wrong (B): RSUs can be granted to any employee, not only executives; there is no SEC restriction limiting RSU eligibility.
Wrong (C): RSUs are not exercised; they automatically convert to shares upon vesting with no expiration window.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3133;

-- Q3140: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is reflected in the grant-date fair value of the award using pricing models","Is ignored for accounting purposes and has no effect on compensation expense","Requires mark-to-market accounting with fair value remeasurement each period","Is not reflected in fair value but affects the number of awards expected to vest"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 718-10-30-12, a performance condition affecting vesting is not incorporated into the grant-date fair value. Instead, it affects the estimate of awards expected to vest, with compensation expense adjusted each period based on the probability of achieving the revenue target.
Wrong (A): Performance conditions are excluded from fair value; only market conditions (e.g., stock price targets) are reflected in pricing models.
Wrong (B): Performance conditions directly affect expense through the vesting probability estimate and cannot be ignored.
Wrong (C): Mark-to-market accounting applies to liability-classified awards, not equity-classified awards with performance conditions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3140;

-- Q3141: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both conditions are treated identically and all previously recognized expense is reversed","Performance condition failure requires expense reversal, but market condition expense is not reversed","Neither condition affects expense because stock option expense is based solely on the service period","Market condition failure triggers expense reversal while performance condition failure does not"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 718, performance condition failure requires reversal of all previously recognized expense because probability is estimated each period. Market condition failure does not trigger reversal because the probability is incorporated into grant-date fair value through option pricing models (e.g., Monte Carlo simulation), so the cost is already risk-adjusted.
Wrong (A): ASC 718 treats performance and market conditions differently; identical treatment is incorrect.
Wrong (C): Both types of conditions affect expense recognition, though through different mechanisms (quantity vs. unit fair value).
Wrong (D): This reverses the correct treatment; performance conditions trigger reversal, not market conditions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3141;

-- Q3147: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expensed immediately as research and development costs in the period acquired","Recognized as an indefinite-lived intangible asset until completed or abandoned","Included in goodwill as part of the residual acquisition premium","Capitalized and amortized over a standard 10-year useful life from acquisition"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 805-20-25-4, IPR&D acquired in a business combination is recognized at fair value as a separate indefinite-lived intangible asset. It is not amortized but is tested for impairment annually until the project is completed (reclassified as finite-lived) or abandoned (written off).
Wrong (A): Pre-ASC 805 guidance required immediate expensing, but current standards require asset recognition at fair value.
Wrong (C): IPR&D meeting the recognition criteria must be separately identified, not subsumed into goodwill.
Wrong (D): IPR&D is classified as indefinite-lived during development; amortization begins only upon project completion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3147;

-- Q3156: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Incremental cost is $450,000 (50,000 x $9) because the original grant is completely replaced","Incremental cost is $50,000 (50,000 x ($3 - $8)) reflecting the reduced fair value from lower price","Incremental cost is $300,000 (50,000 x ($9 - $3)), the excess of modified over original fair value","No incremental cost because underwater options have zero value and repricing restores original terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 718, a repricing is a modification measured as the excess of the modified award''s fair value ($9) over the original award''s fair value ($3) at the modification date: ($9 - $3) x 50,000 = $300,000. The original $8 grant-date fair value continues to be recognized, and the $300,000 incremental cost is recognized over the remaining 2-year vesting period.
Wrong (A): This treats the repricing as a new grant, ignoring that the original award''s expense continues to be recognized.
Wrong (B): The calculation direction is wrong; the modified fair value exceeds the original, producing incremental cost, not savings.
Wrong (D): Underwater options retain time value, and ASC 718 requires recognizing the incremental compensation cost of any modification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3156;

-- Q3157: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignored in the acquisition accounting because the relationship predates the combination","Included in the goodwill calculation as part of the acquisition premium residual","Effectively settled with any gain or loss recognized separately from the acquisition","Carried forward at book value with no adjustment required upon acquisition"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 805-10-25-20, preexisting relationships between the acquirer and acquiree are effectively settled as part of the business combination. Any settlement gain or loss is recognized separately in earnings and excluded from the purchase price allocation, ensuring acquisition accounting reflects only the combination itself.
Wrong (A): Preexisting relationships must be accounted for in the acquisition; ignoring them would misstate the purchase price allocation.
Wrong (B): Settlement gains or losses on preexisting relationships are recognized in earnings, not included in goodwill.
Wrong (D): The relationship is settled at fair value, not carried forward at book value, upon the acquisition date.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3157;

-- Q3161: Advanced Consolidations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They artificially increase consolidated revenue and cost of goods sold balances","They create tax complications that prevent accurate consolidated tax reporting","GAAP prohibits all related-party transactions between parents and subsidiaries","They are internal transfers within a single economic entity, not external transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 810-10-45-1, consolidated financial statements present the parent and subsidiaries as a single economic entity. Intercompany sales must be eliminated because they are internal transfers, not transactions with outside parties, and including them would overstate consolidated revenue and expenses.
Wrong (A): While intercompany sales do inflate revenue, this describes the effect rather than the underlying reason for elimination.
Wrong (B): Tax complications are a separate issue; elimination is required for financial reporting accuracy, not tax reasons.
Wrong (C): GAAP does not prohibit related-party transactions; it requires their elimination in consolidation and disclosure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3161;

-- Q3163: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$80,000 entirely to the parent as the controlling interest holder","$80,000 entirely to the noncontrolling interest as the subsidiary''s shareholders","$56,000 to the parent and $24,000 to noncontrolling interest proportionally","$40,000 to each party split equally regardless of ownership percentage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-45-18, unrealized profit from upstream intercompany sales is allocated between controlling and noncontrolling interests based on ownership percentages. The $80,000 markup is split 70% to the parent ($56,000) and 30% to NCI ($24,000) because the subsidiary originated the sale.
Wrong (A): Allocating entirely to the parent applies to downstream sales, not upstream sales where the subsidiary is the seller.
Wrong (B): NCI bears only its proportional share of the elimination, not the entire amount.
Wrong (D): Equal allocation ignores the 70/30 ownership split and has no basis under ASC 810.$EXPL$
