-- Q3168: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are not eliminated because they represent actual cash transfers between separate legal entities","Only the income statement effect is eliminated; the cash flow statement shows the full dividend amount","They are eliminated across all statements because the consolidated entity cannot pay dividends to itself","They are eliminated on the balance sheet only; the income and cash flow statements report them normally"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-45, intercompany dividends are eliminated because, from the consolidated perspective, an entity cannot pay dividends to itself. The parent''s dividend income is reversed on the income statement, intercompany receivables and payables are eliminated on the balance sheet, and the cash flow statement excludes internal transfers per ASC 230.
Wrong (A): While cash physically transfers between legal entities, consolidated statements treat them as a single entity, requiring elimination.
Wrong (B): Elimination applies to all three financial statements, not just the income statement.
Wrong (D): The elimination is comprehensive across all financial statements, not limited to the balance sheet alone.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3168;

-- Q3169: Advanced Consolidations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Eliminate the $60,000 gain and reduce excess depreciation by $12,000 annually","Eliminate the $60,000 intercompany gain only with no depreciation adjustment needed","Eliminate the $260,000 transfer price as intercompany revenue in consolidation","No consolidation adjustment is needed because the subsidiary now owns the equipment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 810-10-45-1, the $60,000 intercompany gain ($260,000 minus $200,000) is eliminated. Additionally, the subsidiary''s excess depreciation of $12,000 per year ($60,000 over 5 years) is reversed to align with the consolidated cost basis. The depreciation correction effectively recognizes the deferred profit over the asset''s remaining life.
Wrong (B): Eliminating only the gain without adjusting depreciation would leave the subsidiary depreciating an inflated cost basis on the consolidated books.
Wrong (C): The elimination targets the intercompany profit, not the full transfer price, which includes the asset''s book value.
Wrong (D): Intercompany asset transfers require elimination regardless of which entity holds the asset after the transfer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3169;

-- Q3171: Advanced Consolidations [easy/L1]
